--
KNOWNBUG: [class.union]/2 -- a union may have a user-defined constructor.  CBMC
raises "invalid implicit conversion from 'signed int' to 'union U'" instead of
calling the union's constructor U(int) (even for direct construction `U u(5)`),
so a union is effectively treated as POD with no user constructors.  This is the
storage shape of std::optional/std::variant (a union with a constructor that
initialises one member), and is why `std::optional<int> o = 5; o.value()` fails.
A companion bug -- anonymous-union member initialization in a constructor
init-list -- is fixed (see cpp11_anonymous_union_member_init); this one (union
*constructors*) is still open.
